\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Forecasting Annual U.S.
 Honey Bee Colony Numbers Using Time Series Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha (903644479), Elisabeth Gutierrez-Zaheer (903620049), Vincent
 Kofi Amedekah (903743705)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
December 6, 2022
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Problem Statement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing single
\noindent
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

North America is home to nearly 4,500 species of bees [1].
 Most species are solitary, but there are 49 known species of primitive
 eusocial bumble bees in the United States.
 41 of these species are also found in Canada, and an additional 11 species
 are found in Mexico.
 In 1622, the highly eusocial western honey bee (“Apis mellifera”) was introduce
d to North America from Europe and Africa [2], and it is the only species
 of honey bee in North America.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Honey bees and other plant pollinators are vital to the health of agricultural
 and plant ecosystems.
 In fact, honey bees pollinate many of the fruits, vegetables, and nuts
 the human population grows and consumes.
 75% to 80% of the world’s crops depend on pollinators like the honey bee
 [3], and the need for honey bee pollination services has increased as the
 demand for insect-pollinated food has risen.
 Unfortunately, honey bees in the United States have a high mortality rate.
 “…over the last decade, the U.S.
 annual honey bee colony mortality rate has fluctuated between 30-45%, with
 some beekeepers losing half or more of their colonies every year” [4].
 Nonetheless, colony loss rates remain separate from honey bee population
 decline.
 The honey bee population (i.e., the overall number of colonies) has remained
 steady over the years, but the beekeeping and agricultural industry do
 face challenges [5].
 There is still notable concern for the overall health of honey bee colonies,
 which has led to the establishment of nonprofit organizations, such as
 “The Bee Informed Partnership”, to help improve the health and long-term
 sustainability of honey bees.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

In the United States, honey bees have such a high mortality rate due to
 a complex suite of factors.
 “Several critical factors threaten honey bees including poor nutrition
 due to land use change, pesticide exposure, and introduced pests and diseases”
 [4].
 Varroa mites, which were introduced into the United States in the 1980s,
 are one of the major external parasitic threats that continue to negatively
 impact US honey bee colonies.
 Elevated pathogens and pesticides also have a substantial influence on
 honey bees, with a wide array of pesticides being detected in wax and pollen
 [4].
 These external threats then make honey bee colonies more susceptible to
 serious diseases, like “Colony Collapse Disorder” (CCD), which is a phenomenon
 that occurs when thriving colonies empty within a few weeks because many
 worker bees disappear [6].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Moreover, climate change continues to have intensifying weight on honey
 bee mortality, especially due to extreme temperatures.
 To elaborate, honey bees are active when it is 60.8 degrees Fahrenheit,
 but they stop flying when the temperature falls below 50 degrees.
 If the temperature drops below 28 degrees, then they die within one hour
 [7].
 In a survey performed from 2014 to 2019, the mortality rate for bees was
 53.5% during the winter [8].
 While honey bees can survive in both high and low temperatures, they will
 still die if there are prolonged instances of substantially high or low
 temperatures because they suffer severe water loss and weakened muscles
 to fly [7].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Finally, air quality can damage honey bee colonies.
 A 2020 study performed in India demonstrated that the number of bees visiting
 flowers reduced significantly in areas with elevated levels of pollution,
 while the opposite was true in areas where pollution was lower [9].
 The study identified high and low pollution areas by investigating the
 levels of particulate matter (PM), consisting of sulfate, nitrates, ammonia,
 sodium chloride, black carbon, mineral dust, and water, in the air [9].
 Note that elevated levels of PM2.5 means that the PM is small enough to
 enter the systems of humans and animals [10].
 Other environmental exposures such as ozone, nitrogen, and sulfur dioxide
 can also have serious outcomes on honey bee health, and elevated levels
 of pollutants can cause bees to die.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Consequently, such highly volatile dynamics in honey bee mortality can lead
 to unpredictable outcomes for our country's ecosystems and agricultural
 industry.
 Colony loss directly affects the honey bee population, and a potential
 decrease in the number of honey bee colonies could negatively impact crop
 production yields.
 While beekeepers can help to create more colonies, replacing colonies still
 comes at a great cost, and it should not be underappreciated [4].
 Over the last 20 years, annual loss rates remain high, so beekeepers are
 consistently under pressure to offset these losses by creating new colonies
 [11].
 Instead of reactionary decision making based on constant fluctuations in
 the honey bee population, it could help beekeepers, farmers, etc.
 to have insight into how many colonies they can expect to have in the future.
 This would enable the appropriate policy makers to improve assessments
 of local and regional habitat suitability and ultimately make more informed
 decisions.
 If these groups had advanced knowledge of substantial colony loss in the
 future, then perhaps they could take steps in the present to mitigate some
 of the problems through targeted initiatives.
 Therefore, the goal of this project is to utilize past data to help build
 a machine learning model that forecasts annual honey bee colony numbers
 for different states in the U.S.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Literature Survey
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

As explained, the topic of honey bee population loss is an important one.
 Most of the research being done is focused on analyzing data collected
 through surveys.
 The “Bee Informed Partnership” created the “Colony Loss and Management
 Survey,” which monitors managed honey bee colony loss rates and identifies
 risk factors and protective measures [12].
 Furthermore, the “National Honey Bee Survey” is a comprehensive examination
 of colony health throughout the United States, and it helps document honey
 bee diseases, pests, and pathogens [13].
 The data collected through these surveys is valuable to both beekeepers
 and researchers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

For instance, a review published in EcoHealth [14] utilized data collected
 through surveys from individual beekeepers to study the drivers of decline
 in bee colonies.
 The study was conducted by identifying socioeconomic, political, and environmen
tal factors affecting the bee industry, and various models were run to analyze
 bee colony numbers.
 It was found that how the bees are managed, along with pathogens and pesticides
, were significant contributors to the decline of the bee colonies.
 The approach used in the study can be improved by adding more datasets
 and evaluating data across the entire United States to get a more extensive
 view of the factors impacting honey bee colonies, such as weather and air
 quality, which our project intends to do.
 Most importantly, our primary goal is to create a machine learning model
 to forecast future honey bee colony numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

However, there are other studies that have taken a more theoretical approach.
 Chen et al.
 [15] formulated theoretical mathematical equations to study the impact
 on the number of honeybee colonies, and they considered several factors
 as variables, such as diseases, pesticides, and food availability.
 Though, the equations were only based on theory, and they were not validated
 to for practical use.
 The authors did state that the use of machine learning to validate the
 theoretical models was necessary since their approach lacked the use of
 real data.
 Our project will use machine learning modeling to validate real historical
 time series data, and we’ll consider various factors when building our
 machine learning models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Regardless, there are still only a few studies related to forecasting honey
 bee activity or colony numbers.
 Ngo et al.
 [16] used temporal convolutional neural networks to forecast the population
 daily loss rate of honey bee colonies, and they determined warning levels
 based on the predictions.
 Yet, their project was narrowly focused on individual honey bee colonies
 since they collected experimental data from a honey bee farm located in
 Taiwan to help construct their algorithm.
 Moreover, Rumkee et al.
 [17] simulated the effects of pesticides on different honey bee life stages
 using the BEEHAVE model.
 “The BEEHAVE model simulates the development of a honey bee colony and
 its foraging of nectar and pollen in different landscapes” [18].
 Matthias et al.
 [19] was another study that utilized the BEEHAVE system.
 The authors found the increase in failure of bee colonies were attributed
 to pathogens, forage availability, and potentially pesticides.
 To sum up, all these initiatives focused on how individual honey bee colonies
 responded to external stressors, specifically pesticide exposure and pathogens.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Our study, on the other hand, will have a broader focus than the research
 projects outlined in the previous paragraph since we will try to predict
 the annual honey bee population for individual states, as well as the aggregate
d numbers of these states.
 In addition, we will try to consider several factors that are correlated
 with increased honey bee mortality, including pesticides, diseases, pests,
 temperature, precipitation, and air quality.
 The aim of this project is to account for the factors that have a potential
 impact on honey bees to make more informed predictions.
 Finally, we will experiment with different machine learning models to see
 which approach produces the best forecasts of future honey bee colony numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Data Sources
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

For our project, we produced two separate data sources to experiment with
 different kinds of methodologies because we wanted to examine which ones
 produced the best predictive results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Dataset A
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

To easily distinguish between the two datasets we utilized, we referred
 to the dataset described in this subsection as “Dataset A.” The primary
 segment of the data was retrieved from the National Agricultural Statistics
 Service (NASS), which is part of the United States Department of Agriculture
 (USDA) [20].
 It contained annual honey bee colony numbers, which we treated as our response
 variable, for various states from 1987 to 2021.
 Important to note, this was the most complete and expansive dataset on
 honey bee populations that could be obtained from the NASS.
 The NASS creates its datasets through a wide-ranging Census conducted every
 5 years, or through more constrained quarterly or annual surveys.
 Our group decided to focus on the data collected from the surveys.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Unfortunately, due to the limitations of these surveys, there were 11 states
 that did not have observations for every year.
 Instead, some of these states had their honey bee numbers grouped together
 in a separate series labelled 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

other states.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Since the set of states that made up 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

other states
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 were widely dissimlar and ever changing, we decided to remove this series
 from the dataset entirely.
 Furthermore, any states that did not have continuous observations for the
 entire time horizon were also dropped.
 Therefore, the final version of Dataset A was composed of separate time
 series of annual honey bee colony numbers for 39 states from 1987 to 2021,
 and we assumed these 39 states were a fair representation of the United
 States as a whole.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

With this setup, Dataset A was often treated as a hierarchal time series.
 The summation of the honey bee population in a given year across the 39
 states represented the “top” of the hierarchy, or the most aggregate level
 of the data.
 So, the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-th observation of the aggregated time series was denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1987,\ldots,2021$
\end_inset

.
 Obviously, this top-level series was also disaggregated into the 39 separate
 time series, where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j,t}$
\end_inset

 denoted the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-th observation corresponding to the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

-th series for 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,39$
\end_inset

.
 For any time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, the observations for all the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 time series summed to the aggregated time series, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}=y_{1,t}+\ldots+y_{39,t}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Moreover, we generated annual data related to climate and air quality for
 the 39 states.
 These features were frequently combined with the annual honey bee colony
 numbers to create multivarite time series for each state and help improve
 the predictive power of the models employing Dataset A.
 First, the climate data was retrieved from the National Centers for Environment
al Information (NCEI) [21].
 The dataset consisted of monthly weather measurements at the state level
 from 1895 to the present.
 For our project, the maximum temperature, minimum temperature, and precipitatio
n measures were retained.
 During the preprocessing, the monthly data was rolled up to the annual
 level for each state by taking the monthly maximum and minimum temperatures,
 respectively, over the 12-month readings, while the annual precipitation
 was calculated by adding the 12-month readings.
 Next, the air quality data was obtained from the United States Environmental
 Protection Agency (EPA) [22].
 The annual summary data per county was extracted for the years of 1987
 to 2021, and the data was preprocessed to merge all the various data files
 sets into one dataset.
 For each feature column on air quality, the data was grouped into states
 and counties, and the aggregate mean for the 39 states was calculated using
 the appropriate county values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Dataset B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Moving on, we referred to the dataset described in this subsection as “Dataset
 B.” This dataset contained annual honey bee data for 45 states and “other
 states” (an aggregation of data from Alaska, Delaware, Nevada, New Hampshire,
 and Rhode Island) from 2015 to 2021.
 Similar to the other dataset, much of the data in this dataset was obtained
 from the NASS quarterly survey data [20].
 Though, Dataset B did not only include annual data on honey bee populations.
 It also contained data on various features that typically impact honey
 bee mortality, such as the percentage of honey bee colonies affected by
 renovation, diseases, pests, pesticides, and “other causes”.
 The quarterly data for each state was aggregated to the annual level by
 taking the average of the four quarters for each feature.
 We also obtained annual data on the percentage of beekeepers and honey
 bee colonies exclusive to each state, along with a weighted average of
 total colony loss, from the “Bee Informed Partnership” [23].
 All these additional features were been combined with the annual data on
 air quality and climate to produce a large set of independent variables.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Now, the main goal of our project was to generate a strong predictive model
 to forecast annual honey bee colony numbers in the future.
 To accomplish this objective, we wanted to utilize both datasets to generate
 and test several models, and then select the model with the best predictive
 performance.
 Yet, in order to properly compare models built using either of the two
 datasets, the set of states in both needed to match.
 So, we eliminated data corresponding to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

other states
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and the 6 additional states found in Dataset B (i.e., Connecticut, Maryland,
 Massachusetts, New Mexico, Oklahoma, and South Carolina).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Finally, the structure of Dataset B was more in line with longitudinal or
 panel data.
 As mentioned, it not only had data for our response variable, but it also
 consisted of data for various independent variables.
 The dataset contained a collection of various quantities obtained across
 multiple observational units, in this case the 39 individual states, that
 were assembled over even intervals in time and ordered chronologically.
 So, for each observational unit 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in Dataset B, we had a time series 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left(x_{t},y_{t}\right):t=2015,\ldots,2021\right\} $
\end_inset

 with a total of 
\begin_inset Formula $T=7$
\end_inset

 years where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

 was a 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times1$
\end_inset

 feature vector and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 was the response variable, and we had a total of 
\begin_inset Formula $T=7$
\end_inset

 years.
 Therefore, we could write the data for all our 
\begin_inset Formula $m=39$
\end_inset

 observational units compactly in matrix form 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left(\mathbf{X}^{i},\mathbf{y}^{i}\right):i=1,\ldots,39\right\} $
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}^{i}$
\end_inset

 was a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times n$
\end_inset

, written as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{X}^{i}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\left(x_{1}^{i}\right)^{\text{T}}\\
\left(x_{2}^{i}\right)^{\text{T}}\\
\vdots\\
\left(x_{T}^{i}\right)^{\text{T}}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}^{i}$
\end_inset

 was our standard 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times1$
\end_inset

 feature vector, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{y}^{i}$
\end_inset

 was a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of response variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Similarities and Differences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Now, for both of the datasets, we collected data for 39 U.S.
 states, or "units", over fixed periods of time.
 In addition, the two datasets were "fixed and balanced" since each state
 was tracked over the same number of time periods, and the same set of states
 were tracked in each time period.
 For each unit, we included a set of explanatory variables, which all changed
 across time periods.
 We wanted to make use of these measured features to help forecast the response
 variable, which was the total number of bee colonies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Yet, after aligning the set of states, there were two major differences
 between Datasets A and B: Dataset A had a larger time horizon than Dataset
 B, while Dataset B contained more explanatory variables.
 To elaborate, the time frame of Dataset A consisted of 35 years, while
 the time frame of Dataset B was only 7 years.
 Dataset B, on the other hand, contained features that were not part of
 Dataset A.
 As mentioned previously, these additional features should have some impact
 on honey bee colony numbers.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Above all, our goal was to choose and fit models that were appropriate for
 whichever dataset was deployed.
 In fact, the differences in structure between the two datasets influenced
 the methodologies that we implemented.
 The number of time periods and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

units
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in Dataset A were almost equal, and Dataset A had a longer time frame.
 So, we experimented with time series methods that performed better under
 these circumstances by fitting them on Dataset A.
 With Dataset B, there was a small number of time periods, and the number
 of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

units
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 was much larger.
 This meant that regression models more suited for panel datasets were fitted
 using Dataset B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Methodologies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Prior to model fitting and evaluation, our group performed exploratory data
 analysis on both datasets to help inform our decisions in regards to data
 preparation, variable selection, and model building.
 Then, using both datasets, we experimented with several models to see which
 one produced the best predictive results.
 In the subsequent subsections, we explain some of our notable findings
 about the datasets, and we outline the different modeling approaches we
 tested.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Exploratory Data Analysis - Dataset A
\end_layout

