\begin_layout Subsubsection
Statistical Tests
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Still, we felt that it was important to conduct a few statistical tests
 on the different time series before making any final decisions about feature
 selection.
 It is important to note that all of the statistical tests outlined below
 were conducted on the aggregated time series of the response variable (
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=y_{1,t}+\ldots+y_{39,t}$
\end_inset

) and the aggregated time series of the various climate and air quality
 features.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

First, we performed the Granger causality test for different sets of variables.
 When constructing models related to time series, like Vector Autoregression
 (VAR), it was important that each of the time series in the system influenced
 each other.
 With the Granger causality test, we tried to determine which time series
 had a p-value greater than 0.05.
 This meant the series did not influence the system, so it could potentially
 be removed from consideration when building our models.
 Below are some of the notable results of the Granger causality tests
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

days_pm2.5
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 had a p-value of 0.1576 > 0.05 for Granger cause of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

temperature_min
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Quotes eld
