\begin_layout Subsection
Vector Autoregression (VAR) - Dataset A
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

With Dataset A, one approach was to use the aggregated time series of the
 remaining features to help forecast the annual honey bee colony numbers
 of the aggregated time series (
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=y_{1,t}+\ldots+y_{39,t}$
\end_inset

).
 Because we were dealing with multivariate time series, we decided to experiment
 with the Vector Autoregression (VAR) model, which can be used when two
 or more time series influence each other.
 In the VAR model, each variable was modeled as a linear combination of
 past values of itself and the past values of other variables in the system.
 Since we had several time series that influenced each other, VAR was modeled
 as a system of equations with one equation per variable or time series.
 For example, the second order VAR(2) model for two variables includes up
 to two lags for each variable, and it can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{l}
Y_{1,t}=\alpha_{1}+\beta_{11,1}Y_{1,t-1}+\beta_{12,1}Y_{2,t-1}+\beta_{11,2}Y_{1,t-2}+\beta_{12,2}Y_{2,t-2}+\epsilon_{1,t}\\
Y_{2,t}=\alpha+\beta_{21,1}Y_{1,t-1}+\beta_{22,1}Y_{2,t-1}+\beta_{21,2}Y_{1,t-2}+\beta_{22,2}Y_{2,t-2}+\epsilon_{2,t}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

To select the order (P) of the VAR model, we iteratively fit several orders
 of the VAR model, and we picked the order that produced a model with the
 lowest Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) score.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Continuing on, the VAR model required the separate time series to be stationary,
 meaning the statistical properties of each time series (e.g., mean, variance,
 etc.) remained constant overtime.
 So, we performed the Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF) test, one of the most
 commonly used statistical tests when analyzing the stationarity of a time
 series, on each of the remaining aggregated time series.
 The first iteration of the ADF test showed that most of the time series
 were not stationary.
 However, all the time series were stationary after first-differencing.
 So, after applying a first-difference adjustment to the remaining aggregated
 time series, we were able to build the VAR model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) - Dataset A
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

The long short-term memory (LSTM) recurrent neural network was another methodolo
gy that we tested using Dataset A.
 Though, instead of aggregating the data across the 39 states and producing
 a single aggregate forecast, like we did with VAR, we utilized the separate
 data of all 39 states to produce individual forecasts of annual honey bee
 colony numbers for each of them.
 We chose LSTM because it is effective in learning the dependencies in a
 sequence of predictions.
 By using its memory cells to store relevant information, and gates that
 address the continuous read, write, and reset of the memory cells, it can
 handle both linear and nonlinear time series.
 It also offered the capability to optimize internally using the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Adam
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 optimization algorithm for each of its parameters and equations.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The Adam optimization algorithm is an extension to stochastic gradient descent
 that has recently seen broader adoption for deep learning applications...
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 [24].
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

In our implementation of LSTM, we decided to use the rectified linear unit
 (ReLU) as our activation function, one of the most popular ones for neural
 networks.
 ReLU was chosen over Sigmoid and Tanh because those activation functions
 can suffer saturation over time and impact the learning ability of the
 neural network.
 The ReLU function was applied to the output gate when the cell state (long-term
 memory) and the hidden state (short-term memory) were combined to generate
 the output.
 ReLU transformed the weighted input to the output gate in order to learn
 both linear and non-linear patterns in the data.
 Then, it generated a postive and approximately linear output using 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{relu}(x)=\max(0,x)$
\end_inset

.
 Furthermore, we employed the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Dropout
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 probabilistic regularization method to reduce overfitting in the neural
 network.
 During each training sample, Dropout was fed a specific probabaility of
 20%, and it deactivated different inputs and recurrent connections to the
 LSTM units when the weights were updated.
 This allowed the LSTM units to generalize since they were less sensitive
 to specific weights.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

To obtain the best fit model, mean squared error was chosen as the primary
 loss function.
 Also, the same features that were selected to build the VAR model were
 used in our LSTM approach (i.e., 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unhealthy_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

very_unhealthy_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

hazardous_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

days_no2
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

days_ozone
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

days_pm2.5
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).
 With the use of its architecture, LSTM was able to account for the trends
 and seasonalities that may have been present in the different time series
 for the feature and response variables.
 The retained data was structured in matrix format, 10 training epochs were
 used to create and fit the model, and we used a lag of 3 years as the rolling
 window to perform cross-validation.
 Then, LSTM determined the model with the minimum mean squared error, and
 it saved this 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

best
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 version with a call back function.
 Finally, we treated each of the 39 states individually to obtain the best
 LSTM model for each one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Exploratory Data Analysis - Dataset B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

In our project proposal, one of the methodolgies we proposed was the pooled
 ordinary least squares (OLS) regression model, which was directly tied
 to a linear panel data model 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}=x_{t}\beta+\epsilon_{t}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
 With the panel data setup described in the Section 3.2, we were able to
 write the model for an observational unit 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in matrix form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{y}^{i}=\mathbf{X}^{i}\beta+\mathbf{\epsilon}^{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 was the 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times1$
\end_inset

 population parameter that we want to estimate (same for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

), and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\epsilon}^{i}$
\end_inset

 was the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of error terms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

However, with linear a model, we needed to make normality assumptions about
 the response data we were trying to estimate.
 If the response data was not close to fitting the normal distribution,
 then the model could give biased results.
 So, we first examined the histogram of the response variable:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename Dataset B_Figure 1.png
	width 12cm
	height 8cm

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Clearly, the response variable was not normally distributed.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

So, we tried to alleviate this problem by trying a Box-Cox transformation.
 A Box-Cox transformation is a logarithmic transformation that stretches
 out the smaller range to enlarge its variability and shrinks the larger
 range to reduce its variability 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
t(y)=\frac{\left(y^{\lambda}-1\right)}{\lambda}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is the vector of responses and 
\begin_inset Formula $t(y)$
\end_inset

 is the transformed vector.
 The idea is to find the best 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $t(y)$
\end_inset

 can become closer to the normal distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

After applying a Box-Cox transformation on the response variable, we found
 the optimal 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{*}\approx-0.1487$
\end_inset

.
 Still, we needed to verify if the transformed data was approximately normally
 distributed:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename Dataset B_Figure 2.png
	width 12cm
	height 8cm

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The figure on the left is the plot of the KDE of the original data, and
 the figure on the right is the plot of the KDE of the transformed data.
 Unfortunately, the transformed data was not normally distributed, and this
 notion was corroborated after examining the Q-Q plot:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename Dataset B_Figure 3.png
	width 12cm
	height 8cm

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Therefore, we decided to change one of the original methodolgies we outlined
 in our project proposal.
 Since the response variable was not normally distributed, even after applying
 a Box-Cox transformation, we felt it was best not to experiment with linear
 panel data regression models, like pooled OLS.
 Instead, we thought a non-linear pandel data regression model would be
 more appropriate, and this is further discussed in the next subsection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Mixed-Effects Random Forest (MERF) - Dataset B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Dataset B was entirely comprised of longitudinal clusters since there were
 multiple measures of each state over time.
 So, each of the 39 states represented a separate cluster.
 With this clustered data, we wanted to learn about the common effects across
 all states, but also account for the unique characteristics of each one.
 It was difficult to generate a separate model for each state because any
 given model did not have enough training data.
 If we created one global model to learn across all states, then it would
 not be able to learn anything distinctive about particular states.
 Also, one-hot encoding the states was an option, but it has been documented
 that high cardinality categorical variables as features can lead to poor
 model performance.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Thus, a mixed-effects model was chosen for Dataset B.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

In a mixed-effect model, each cluster gets a random effect that is learned
 but drawn from a prior that is itself learned from the data
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 [25].
 Mixed-effects linear modeling is a classic technique, but as was shown
 in the previous section, it was not a good idea to fit a linear model on
 Dataset B.
 Instead, we decided to combine random forests with linear random effects,
 which is known as a mixed-effects random forest (MERF) method.
 MERF is an extenstion of the random forest model to the case of clustered
 data, and it is implemented using a standard random forest algorithm withing
 the framework of the expectation-maximization (EM) algorithm [26].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

The MERF method assumes a generative model of the form: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=f(X)+b_{i}Z+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is the response variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a general non-linear function, 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 contains the 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 fixed-effect features, 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 contains the 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 random-effect features, 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 is independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) noise distributed as 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0,\sigma_{e}^{2})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is the cluster index (there are 39 clusters in Dataset B), and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}$
\end_inset

 contains the random effect coefficients.
 Note there are different coefficients per cluster 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, but they are assumed to be drawn from the same distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0,\sigma_{b})$
\end_inset

 [25].
 Generally, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 can be any non-linear regression model, but we focused on utilizing a random
 forest, just as in the paper by Hajjem, Bellavance, & Larocque [26].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

The MERF Python Package used the EM algorithm to fit four parameters: 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 39 clusters, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{b}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\epsilon}$
\end_inset

.
 The generalized log-likelihood (GLL) was used as a measure of training
 loss, and it was computed at every iteration.
 As the GLL decreased, the fit improved, and once it flattened, the fit
 converged.
 Just like a classic random forest model, the MERF was fairly robust to
 overfitting.
 Finally, the fit MERF was used to predict data in the 39 clusters the model
 saw in training: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y}=f(X)+b_{i}Z
\]

\end_inset

where the prediction included the random effect correction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Feature Selection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Now, the air quality features retained for the VAR and LSTM models (i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unhealthy_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

very_unhealthy_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

hazardous_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

days_no2
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

days_ozone
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

days_pm2.5
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) were also considered for the MERF model, while all the other air quality
 and temperature features were disregarded.
 Yet, we still needed to identify the most relevant features among the set
 of variables that usually have an affect on honey bee mortality or colony
 numbers.
 Luckily, random forests are often used for feature selection for other
 machine learning models.
 The reason is because the tree-based strategies used by random forests
 naturally ranks features by how well they improve the purity of the node.
 Each tree of the random forest can calculate the importance of a feature
 according to its ability to increase the pureness of the leaves.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The higher the increment in leaves purity, the higher the importance of
 the feature.
 This is done for each tree, then is averaged among all the trees and, finally,
 normalized to 1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 [27].
 So, we first trained a standard random forest model using the large set
 of features.
 Then, we identified the most important features, created a new dataset
 with the limited set of features, and trained the MERF model on the new
 dataset.
 Finally, because all features for each state changed over time, they were
 used as both fixed-effects (
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 matrix) and random-effects (
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 matrix) in the construction of the MERF model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Evaluations and Final Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Overview
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

To assess the performances of the different models we tested, it was important
 to evaluate their accuracy using genuine forecasts.
 We were unable to randomly assign data into training and test sets because
 it made no sense to use future data to forecast values in the past.
 We needed to consider the temporal dependencies between the observations
 in our two datasets.
 So, particular care was taken in splitting the data to prevent data leakage.
 With both datasets, our data was organized chronologically, and then we
 picked 2016 as the point in time to split our data.
 All data prior to and including 2016 was always used to fit our models,
 while data occurring after 2016 was withheld for validation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

More specifically, data from 2017 to 2021 was used for time series cross-validat
ion (TSCV) in which cross-validation was performed on a rolling basis.
 We primarily implemented the TSCV procedure outlined by Hyndman and Athanasopou
los [28] involving one-step ahead forecasts.
 The series of validation sets consisted of all observations from a single
 year, while the corresponding training sets consisted of all observations
 that occurred in the past.
 As a result, no future observations were used in the construction of forecasts.
 The diagram below illustrates the series of training and validation sets,
 where the blue dots represent the observations in the training sets and
 the orange dots represent the observations in the validation sets.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename TSCV Diagram.png
	width 12cm
	height 6cm

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a given model, the first iteration of TSCV used training data all the
 way up to 2016 to fit the model, a set of forecasted data points for 2017
 was produced, and the accuracy of the forecasted data was measured against
 the validation set of true 2017 data.
 In subsequent iterations, the previous iteration's validation set was added
 to the training data to fit the model, a set of forecasted data points
 one year ahead was produced, and the accuracy of the forecasted data was
 measured against the validation set of true data one year ahead.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Thus, our TSCV process consisted of 5 separate folds since data from every
 individual year between 2017 to 2021 was used for validation.
 The overall forecast accuracy of a specific model was computed by averaging
 over the results generated from the 5 validation sets.
 It is important to note that the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

one-step ahead
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 TSCV procedure was executed for every model we considered.
 This allowed us to reliably compare models that were built using either
 of our two datasets.
 Then, the model with the smallest average forecast accuracy measures was
 chosen to be our best model.
 Finally, we trained our chosen model using the entirety of its corresponding
 dataset, and we estimated the true performance of this final model by evaluatin
g its 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

one-step ahead
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 forecasts on unseen test data from 2022.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Now, when comparing the forecasting performance of different predictive
 models, we wanted to pick the one with the smallest average forecast error,
 as stated above.
 According to Hyndman and Athanasopoulos [28], forecast error is the difference
 between an observed value and its forecast, and it can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e_{T+h}=y_{T+h}-\hat{y}_{T+h|T}
\]

\end_inset

where the training data is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ y_{1},\ldots,y_{T}\right\} $
\end_inset

 and the test data is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ y_{T+1},y_{T+2},\ldots\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 In fact, there were several different measures that we could have used
 to evaluate forecast accuracy.
 Since the forecast errors were on the same scale as the data (i.e., the total
 number of honey bee colonies), it was permissible to use scale-dependent
 accuracy measures.
 The two popular versions we used were root mean squared error (RMSE)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{RMSE}=\sqrt{\text{mean}\left(e_{t}^{2}\right)}
\]

\end_inset

and mean absolute error (MAE) 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{MAE}=\text{mean}\left(|e_{t}|\right)
\]

\end_inset

“A forecast method that minimizes the MAE will lead to forecasts of the
 median, while minimizing RMSE will lead to forecasts of the mean” [28].
 We also needed an accuracy measure to compare the forecast performances
 of models built using Dataset A versus Dataset B.
 So, mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) was another accuracy measure
 we employed: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{MAPE}=\text{mean}\left(|\nicefrac{100e_{t}}{y_{t}}|\right)
\]

\end_inset

This is because “Percentage errors have the advantage of being unit-free,
 and so are frequently used to compare forecast performances between datasets”
 [28].
 Therefore, the ideal 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

best
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 model should minimize the average RMSE, MAE, and MAPE measures computed
 using our 5-fold TSCV procedure.
\end_layout

