\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

First, we needed to perform feature selection.
 Before running the random forest model, we created a correlation coefficient
 matrix to see how the features were related to the response variable and
 one another.
 Any correlation coefficient greater than or equal to 0.2 was displayed in
 the heat map below:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename Results_Figure 6.png
	width 12cm
	height 10cm

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It seems as though 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

colonies_loss_deadout_pct
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pct_beekeepers_exclusive_state
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pct_colonies_exclusive_state
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unhealthy_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 were the features that had the strongest relationship with the response
 variable.
 With 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

colonies_loss_deadout_pct
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, the percentage of colonies affected by 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

deadout loss
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 was negatively correlated with honey bee colony numbers.
 So, this feature and the response variable move in opposite directions,
 which was expected.
 Unfortunately, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pct_beekeepers_exclusive_state
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pct_colonies_exclusive_state
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unhealthy_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 all had directions that were opposite of what was expected.
 To elaborate, we would expect that the percentage of beekeepers and colonies
 exclusive to a state would be positively correlated with honey bee colony
 numbers, while 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unhealthy
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 air quality days would be negatively correlated with honey bee colony numbers.
 However, all the correlations are based on the entirety of Dataset B, meaning
 all the states have been lumped together.
 Ideally, the directional issues with these three features should be sorted
 out by the MERF model when we account for the differences between states.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Next, we utilized the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

feature importance
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 attribute of the random forest algorithm.
 We trained a standard random forest model on years 2015 and 2016, to prevent
 data leakage, using the large set of features and the default parameter
 values.
 Then, we created a bar chart to display the importance of the various features:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename Results_Figure 7.png
	width 12cm
	height 8cm

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Based on the two sets of results that were displayed, we decided to retain
 all of the features with an importance rating of 0.05 and above, along with
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

colonies_loss_deadout_pct
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 based on its correlation with the response variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

Now, the EM algorithm used to fit the MERF model often took quite a long
 time to run, even with a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

stop early
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 threshold and maximum limit on the number of iterations.
 Also, due to the TSCV procedure, the MERF model needed to be fit 5 separate
 times.
 Consequently, it was difficult to perform hyper-parameter tuning because
 of these computing constraints.
 Hence, we only focused on tuning the number of trees for the random forest,
 while the rest of the parameters were set to their default values.
\end_layout

