\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

For the aggregated data from Datase A, the VAR(2) model had an RMSE of 1837.2706,
 and the LSTM model had an RMSE of 4955.18202, which meant the VAR(2) model
 performed better at the aggregated level.
 As a result, we proposed the VAR(2) model for predicting the annual honey
 bee colony numbers for the U.S.
 using the variables 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unhealthy_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

very_unhealthy_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

hazardous_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

days_no2
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

days_ozone
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

days_pm2.5
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 From the explanatory data analyis, the most important series for forecasting
 the colony inventories were the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pm2.5
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

hazardous_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 features.
 Remember, PM2.5 is a measure of the tiny particles in the air that reduce
 visibility when levels are elevated, and higher measures of PM2.5 does have
 a notable impact on the honey bee population.
 The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

hazardous_days
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 feature represented the number of days when the air had elevated pollutants
 beyond the normal threshold.
 Therefore, we can conclude that pollution does have a substantial impact
 on honey bee colonies, specifically in the United States.
 Based on the VAR(2) model forecasts, the honey bee colony population will
 rise to approximately 70,000 in 2023, but it will decrease to approximately
 62,000 in 2025.
 Therefore, we recommend businesses and government authorities to take the
 appropriate measures if they wish to improve the honey bee population in
 the future.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 0.25in
\end_inset

With Dataset B, models such as VAR and LSTM were not suitable because the
 dataset had very few years in the time series in order for these models
 to learn successfully.
 While we did try to fit the mixed-effects random forest on Dataset B, it
 did not produce good forecasts, which was confirmed by its high RMSE values.
 Hence, decided to discard Dataset B from our final considerations.
